module.exports = /*#__PURE__*/JSON.parse('{"id":"avl-tree-insertion","title":"Step-by-Step AVL Insertion","subjectId":"intermediate-dsa-1","nextLessonId":"avl-tree-deletion","prevLessonId":"avl-rotations-and-intuition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Hello! Ready to construct a tree? Today, we will build a complete AVL tree from scratch, one key at a time. We\'ll trace the sequence **40, 20, 10, 25, 30, 22, 50, 42** and see how the tree balance is maintained at every step!"},{"type":"heading","id":"the-golden-rule","level":2,"text":"1. The Golden Rule of AVL Trees"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Before we insert our first key, let\'s establish the most important rule of AVL trees:"},{"type":"callout","style":"important","text":"Do not wait until the end to balance the tree!\\nEvery time you insert a single key, you must walk back up the tree to the root, update the heights, and check the balance factors of all ancestor nodes. The moment you find a node with a Balance Factor of +2 or -2, you perform the rotation immediately before inserting the next key."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This rule guarantees that we never allow a Balance Factor to reach +3 or -3, keeping our operations incredibly efficient."},{"type":"heading","id":"insertion-algorithm-steps","level":2,"text":"2. Step-by-Step Insertion Algorithm"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Every time you insert a value into an AVL tree, follow this exact sequence of steps:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"1. **Standard BST Insertion**: Perform a normal Binary Search Tree insertion. Compare the value with the current node data. Traverse left if smaller, or right if larger. When you find an empty child spot (`nullptr`), create and attach a new leaf Node with `height = 1`."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"2. **Update Height (Backtracking)**: As the recursion unwinds back up the tree, update the height of each ancestor node: `height = 1 + std::max(getHeightOfNode(left), getHeightOfNode(right))`."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"3. **Calculate Balance Factor (BF)**: For the current ancestor node, compute: `balance = getBalanceOfNode(node)` (which is `height(left) - height(right)`)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"4. **Check for Imbalance**: If `balance` is within `[-1, 0, 1]`, the subtree is balanced. If `balance > 1` or `balance < -1`, the node is unbalanced and requires rebalancing."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"5. **Perform the Correct Rotation**:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"· **Left-Left (LL) Case**: If `balance > 1` and `value < left_child->data`, perform a single **Right Rotation** (`LLRoation`).\\n· **Right-Right (RR) Case**: If `balance < -1` and `value > right_child->data`, perform a single **Left Rotation** (`RRrotation`).\\n· **Left-Right (LR) Case**: If `balance > 1` and `value > left_child->data`, perform a **Left-Right Double Rotation** (`LRrotation`).\\n· **Right-Left (RL) Case**: If `balance < -1` and `value < right_child->data`, perform a **Right-Left Double Rotation** (`RLrotation`)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is the C++ code showing the Node template, safe helpers, and the four rotations that we will use to maintain balance during AVL insertion:"},{"type":"code","language":"cpp","code":"class Node {\\npublic:\\n    int data;\\n    Node* left;\\n    Node* right;\\n    int height;\\n    \\n    Node(int value) {\\n        data = value;\\n        left = nullptr;\\n        right = nullptr;\\n        height = 1;\\n    }\\n};\\n\\n// Safe helper to get a node\'s height (nullptr-safe)\\nint getHeightOfNode(Node* node) {\\n    if (node == nullptr) return 0;\\n    return node->height;\\n}\\n\\n// Safe helper to calculate a node\'s balance factor\\nint getBalanceOfNode(Node* node) {\\n    if (node == nullptr) return 0;\\n    return getHeightOfNode(node->left) - getHeightOfNode(node->right);\\n}\\n\\n// 1. LL Case: Right Rotation around y\\nNode* LLRoation(Node* y) {\\n    Node* x = y->left;\\n    Node* T2 = x->right;\\n    \\n    // Rotate\\n    x->right = y;\\n    y->left = T2;\\n    \\n    // Update heights\\n    y->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(y->left), getHeightOfNode(y->right));\\n    x->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(x->left), getHeightOfNode(x->right));\\n    \\n    return x;\\n}\\n\\n// 2. RR Case: Left Rotation around y\\nNode* RRrotation(Node* y) {\\n    Node* x = y->right;\\n    Node* T2 = x->left;\\n    \\n    // Rotate\\n    x->left = y;\\n    y->right = T2;\\n    \\n    // Update heights\\n    y->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(y->left), getHeightOfNode(y->right));\\n    x->height = 1 + max(getHeightOfNode(x->left), getHeightOfNode(x->right));\\n    \\n    return x;\\n}\\n\\n// 3. LR Case: Double Rotation (Left rotate child, then Right rotate parent)\\nNode* LRrotation(Node* root) {\\n    root->left = RRrotation(root->left);\\n    root = LLRoation(root);\\n    return root;\\n}\\n\\n// 4. RL Case: Double Rotation (Right rotate child, then Left rotate parent)\\nNode* RLrotation(Node* root) {\\n    root->right = LLRoation(root->right);\\n    root = RRrotation(root);\\n    return root;\\n}"},{"type":"heading","id":"dynamic-construction-simulation","level":2,"text":"3. Visual Step-by-Step Construction"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Let\'s walk through our insertion sequence. Use the interactive simulator below to follow along with each step, tracing the node positions, balance factors, and rotations!"},{"type":"drawing","id":"avl-insertion-drawing"},{"type":"heading","id":"key-takeaway-skewed-vs-balanced","level":3,"text":"A Remarkable Height Comparison"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Look at the final result: we stored 8 keys in a tree of height 3! If we had inserted these same keys into a normal BST in sorted order, the height would be 8. Searching in our AVL tree will take at most 3 comparisons, compared to 8 in the skewed BST. This is why AVL trees are so powerful!"},{"type":"heading","id":"practice-quiz","level":2,"text":"4. Practice Questions"},{"type":"mcq","question":"What is the core rule of AVL tree insertion?","options":["Insert all nodes first, then balance the tree at the very end.","Balance only the leaf nodes.","Check and rotate immediately after every single insertion if an imbalance is found.","Only balance nodes that contain negative numbers."],"correctAnswerIndex":2},{"type":"mcq","question":"During the insertion of 40, 20, and 10, which node becomes unbalanced, and what is its Balance Factor?","options":["Node 20, BF = +2","Node 40, BF = +2","Node 10, BF = 0","Node 40, BF = -2"],"correctAnswerIndex":1},{"type":"mcq","question":"Can an insertion trigger a rotation at the root node of the tree?","options":["Yes, if updating balance factors upward makes the root node\'s BF reach +2 or -2.","No, rotations can only occur at leaf nodes.","No, the root node is fixed and cannot be rotated.","Yes, but only if the root node is 0."],"correctAnswerIndex":0},{"type":"heading","id":"summary-section","level":2,"text":"5. Summary"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To wrap up: AVL tree construction is a dynamic process. We check balance factors and perform rotations immediately after each insertion. By inserting 40, 20, 10, 25, 30, 22, 50, and 42, we saw how LL, LR, RL, and RR rotations are triggered in real-time. Next, we will translate this conceptual logic into complete C++ code from scratch!"}]}');

